"""Ask the pad which trigger travel window it actually reads.

Space Station calls the control "Stroke Setting" -- a range slider over 0..255
that sets where the trigger starts registering and where it reads full. The
decompile shows it writing to **two different places depending on the pad**, and
neither of them can be settled by reading more C#:

    supportAdaptTrigger == false   ->  TriggerConfig.Start/End
                                       -> configBean.Zero / .End
                                       -> the travel curve block at offset 123
    supportAdaptTrigger == true    ->  AdapterTriggerTypeNormal.Start/End
                                       -> TriggerAdapterConfigBean.Param[0..1]
                                       -> the force-trigger block, 195/196 and
                                          215/216

and on an Apex 5, which is the second case, **the control is hidden entirely**:
the renderer sets `triggerStrokeUsable` to `!supportAdaptTrigger`, so the slider
only ever appears on pads without adaptive triggers. Param[0]/Param[1] are
round-tripped through the blob and never edited by any UI, and the live command
for mode Normal -- `ForceTriggerConfigNormal` -- carries no parameters at all,
just `[side, 0]`. So Flydigi's software neither writes nor sends this window on
this pad, and nothing in their source says whether the *firmware* still honours
one of the two blocks.

That is a question only the hardware answers, so this asks it.

**Degenerate values, not nudges.** "Does a window of 40..200 do anything" needs a
reference input to answer. A window of 0..16 does not: if the pad plays it, the
trigger reaches full scale within the first 6% of its travel and sits there for
the rest, and no recalibration stage can turn that back into a smooth ramp. The
reading is the count of distinct values a slow full sweep produces -- a few if
the window is live, a couple of hundred if it is not.

**The other trigger is the control, in the same run.** Only the chosen side is
given a window; both are swept and both are reported. A difference between two
triggers a hand worked in the same minute is a result. Both coming back
identical means the block is inert, and it means it without needing a separate
baseline run to compare against.

Both arms also force the stored effect to General on *both* sides, so a Lock or
a Race left over from an earlier session cannot be mistaken for a travel window.

    tools/trigger-stroke-probe --baseline     # the pad as it is, both triggers
    tools/trigger-stroke-probe --curve        # the block at 123
    tools/trigger-stroke-probe --param        # Param[0..1] at 195/215
    tools/trigger-stroke-probe --curve --under-race   # the same, under an effect
    tools/trigger-stroke-probe --lock         # what Lock does to the reading

Nothing is ever saved. The profile is written and applied, never committed with
command 166, and is restored before exit -- and an applied-but-unsaved config
dies on the pad's next sleep anyway. The restore is read back and verified
rather than announced; see `restore` for why that is not paranoia.
"""
import argparse
import os
import select
import sys
import time

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))))

from flydigi import device, effects, evdev, identity, mapping

# Which evdev axis each trigger reports on.
AXES = {"left": evdev.ABS_Z, "right": evdev.ABS_RZ}

# A window this narrow cannot be mistaken for anything else: the trigger is
# full-scale before the finger has really moved.
DEFAULT_START = 0
DEFAULT_END = 16


def sweep(seconds, patience=90.0):
    """Wait for a button, then record both triggers for `seconds`.

    Buttons are the liveness signal, for the same reason as in
    `joystick-curve-probe`: a trigger nobody pulled and a trigger the pad has
    flattened to a single value look identical from here. Button events during
    the window prove somebody was working the pad, so "two distinct values" is a
    measurement rather than an empty room.
    """
    path, name = evdev.find_device(vendor=device.VID, axes=True)
    if path is None:
        sys.exit("no Flydigi gamepad on evdev -- press a button to wake the pad")

    seen = {side: [] for side in AXES}
    presses = 0
    with evdev.Reader(path) as reader:
        missing = [s for s, code in AXES.items() if code not in reader.abs]
        if missing:
            sys.exit(f"this node reports no axis for the {', '.join(missing)} "
                     "trigger")
        ranges = {side: (reader.abs[code].minimum, reader.abs[code].maximum)
                  for side, code in AXES.items()}

        while select.select([reader], [], [], 0)[0]:
            reader.read()
        pressed = {c for c, v in reader.keys.items() if v}

        def poll(timeout):
            nonlocal pressed, presses
            if not select.select([reader], [], [], timeout)[0]:
                return False
            synced = reader.read()
            now = {c for c, v in reader.keys.items() if v}
            presses += len(now ^ pressed)
            pressed = now
            return synced

        # Arming on a button alone was wrong here, and cost a run: the triggers
        # are the measurement, so someone told to work the triggers does
        # exactly that and the window never opens. Any movement starts it.
        # Buttons are still counted, because they are what separates "the pad
        # flattened this trigger" from "nobody touched it".
        rest = {side: reader.axes.get(code, 0) for side, code in AXES.items()}

        def stirred():
            return presses > 0 or any(
                abs(reader.axes.get(code, 0) - rest[side]) > 4
                for side, code in AXES.items())

        print(f"{name} ({path}): PULL A TRIGGER to start the {seconds:.0f}s "
              "window, then squeeze BOTH triggers slowly all the way in and "
              "out, a few times, tapping a button as you go")
        giveup = time.time() + patience
        while not stirred() and time.time() < giveup:
            poll(0.2)
        if not stirred():
            sys.exit("nothing moved -- the pad may have slept")
        print("started")

        deadline = time.time() + seconds
        while time.time() < deadline:
            if not poll(0.2):
                continue
            for side, code in AXES.items():
                seen[side].append(reader.axes.get(code, 0))
    print("window closed -- you can stop")
    return seen, presses, ranges


def describe(side, samples, span, presses):
    """One trigger's sweep, as the two numbers that separate the outcomes."""
    lo, hi = span
    distinct = sorted(set(samples))
    print(f"\n{side} trigger  (evdev range {lo}..{hi})")
    if len(distinct) <= 1:
        if presses > 1:
            print("  SILENT: never moved, while buttons were arriving. Either "
                  "it was not pulled or the pad is suppressing it entirely.")
        else:
            print("  VOID: nothing from this trigger and no buttons either.")
        return None
    reach = max(samples)
    floor = min(samples)
    # The whole reading. A pad playing a 0..16 window turns a full sweep into a
    # handful of steps; an untouched trigger resolves the travel in full.
    print(f"  {len(samples)} samples, {len(distinct)} distinct values, "
          f"{floor}..{reach}")
    if hi > lo:
        print(f"  furthest it reported: {reach} of {hi} "
              f"({reach * 100 // (hi - lo)}% of full scale)")
    if hi > lo:
        pinned = sum(1 for s in samples if s >= lo + 0.95 * (hi - lo))
        idle = sum(1 for s in samples if s <= lo + 0.02 * (hi - lo))
        print(f"  {pinned * 100 // len(samples)}% of the sweep pinned at full "
              f"scale, {idle * 100 // len(samples)}% at rest")
    print("  where the sweep landed:")
    for step in range(10):
        band_lo = lo + (hi - lo) * step // 10
        band_hi = lo + (hi - lo) * (step + 1) // 10
        n = sum(1 for s in samples if band_lo <= s < band_hi or
                (step == 9 and s == hi))
        print(f"    {step * 10:3d}-{step * 10 + 10:3d}%  "
              f"{'#' * min(60, n)}{'' if n else '  (empty)'}")
    return {"distinct": len(distinct), "reach": reach}


def verdict(probed, control, side):
    """Compare the two triggers. Only their ratio means anything."""
    print()
    if probed is None or control is None:
        print("INCONCLUSIVE: one of the two triggers produced nothing, so "
              "there is nothing to compare. Run it again and work both.")
        return
    other = "right" if side == "left" else "left"
    probed, control = probed["distinct"], control["distinct"]
    print(f"{side} (window written): {probed} distinct values -- "
          f"{other} (untouched control): {control} distinct values")
    if probed * 4 <= control:
        print(f"VERDICT: LIVE. The {side} trigger resolves a fraction of what "
              f"the {other} one does, which is what a narrow travel window "
              "looks like: full scale reached almost at once and held. The pad "
              "reads this block.")
    elif probed >= control * 0.6:
        print("VERDICT: INERT. Both triggers resolved their travel the same "
              "way, so the window changed nothing. The pad does not read this "
              "block -- writing it moves a slider and nothing else.")
    else:
        print("VERDICT: unclear -- the two differ, but not by the margin a "
              "degenerate window should produce. Most likely one trigger was "
              "worked harder than the other; run it again, sweeping both the "
              "same way.")


def restore(ctrl, cfg_id, original, edited):
    """Put the profile back, and check that it went back.

    **Order matters, and getting it wrong cost two profiles.** The obvious
    shape -- clear the live effects, then write the blob -- leaves the pad
    unrestored while printing that it was restored: two runs ended with the
    probe's own Lock and its 0..128 window still on the pad, and the next run
    read them as the profile's "original" state and put them back again. A
    config write with no live command in front of it restores the same profile
    correctly, which is the difference. hidraw replies go to every reader of the
    node, so the ACKs from those command-81s are still in flight when the write
    handshake starts, and the write reads one of them as its own answer.

    So: blob first, live commands after. And read it back, because a write that
    reports packets sent is not a write the pad kept -- the whole reason this
    probe exists is that an ACK is not evidence.
    """
    sent = mapping.write_config(ctrl, cfg_id, original, old=edited)
    mapping.apply_config(ctrl, cfg_id)
    effects.clear_all(ctrl)
    try:
        back = mapping.read_config(ctrl, cfg_id)
    except mapping.ProtocolError as exc:
        print(f"\nrestored profile {cfg_id + 1} ({sent} packets) but could not "
              f"read it back to check: {exc}")
        return
    intact = all(
        back.trigger_effect(side) == original.trigger_effect(side)
        and back.trigger_curve(side) == original.trigger_curve(side)
        for side in mapping.SIDES)
    if intact:
        print(f"\nrestored profile {cfg_id + 1}, triggers released "
              f"(read back and verified)")
    else:
        print(f"\nWARNING: profile {cfg_id + 1} did NOT go back. It still holds "
              + "; ".join(
                  f"{side} effect {back.trigger_effect(side)[0]}, window "
                  f"{back.trigger_curve(side)['zero']}..{back.trigger_curve(side)['end']}"
                  for side in mapping.SIDES)
              + ". Nothing was saved, so a sleep clears it -- but do not treat "
                "this profile as untouched in the next run.")


def lock_verdict(probed, control, side, stop):
    """Both triggers are locked at `stop`; only one has a window ending there.

    Two readings, and they are independent. The control says what a locked
    trigger reports at its stop with the travel window left alone; the probed
    side says whether a window narrowed onto the lock point rescales that back
    to full. A game reads the number, not the finger.
    """
    print()
    if probed is None or control is None:
        print("INCONCLUSIVE: one of the two triggers produced nothing. Run it "
              "again and pull both right up to the stop.")
        return
    other = "right" if side == "left" else "left"
    print(f"both triggers locked at {stop}. "
          f"{side} (window 0..{stop}): reached {probed['reach']} -- "
          f"{other} (window left alone): reached {control['reach']}")

    # Checked before anything about reach, because it makes reach meaningless.
    # This is what actually happened on an Apex 5 and it was in neither branch
    # below: a locked trigger reports 0 or 255 and nothing in between, which an
    # event-driven axis shows as a handful of events for a whole run.
    if probed["distinct"] <= 3 and control["distinct"] <= 3:
        print(f"VERDICT: Lock makes the trigger DIGITAL. Both sides produced "
              f"{probed['distinct']} and {control['distinct']} distinct values "
              "-- nothing between rest and full. There is no analogue reading "
              "for a travel window to rescale, which is why the two sides match "
              "despite one having a window ending at the lock point. Lock is a "
              "hair trigger in the button sense, not a shortened analogue pull.")
        return

    if control["reach"] >= 250:
        print("The locked trigger still reports full scale on its own, so the "
              "stop is a wall the finger meets after the reading has already "
              "topped out. Nothing to rescale.")
    else:
        print(f"On its own a locked trigger tops out at {control['reach']} of "
              f"255 -- a game pulling it to the stop sees "
              f"{control['reach'] * 100 // 255}% and never more.")

    if probed["reach"] >= control["reach"] + 24:
        print(f"VERDICT: the window APPLIES UNDER LOCK, and it is the fix: "
              f"narrowing travel to the lock point took the reading from "
              f"{control['reach']} back up to {probed['reach']}. Lock plus a "
              "matching Travel end is a hair trigger that still reports full.")
    elif abs(probed["reach"] - control["reach"]) <= 8:
        print("VERDICT: the window did not change what the locked trigger "
              "reports, and the reading stayed analogue. The effect block is "
              "overriding the travel curve -- test an analogue effect with "
              "--curve --under-race before believing that in general.")
    else:
        print("VERDICT: unclear -- they differ, but by less than a rescale "
              "would give. Most likely the two were not pulled equally hard "
              "into the stop. Run it again.")


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description=__doc__,
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    ap.add_argument("--curve", action="store_true",
                    help="write the window into the travel curve block at 123, "
                         "where Space Station puts it on a pad WITHOUT adaptive "
                         "triggers -- zero, end and the two mirrored points")
    ap.add_argument("--param", action="store_true",
                    help="write the window into Param[0..1] of the force-trigger "
                         "block, where Space Station puts it on a pad WITH "
                         "adaptive triggers -- 195/196 and 215/216")
    ap.add_argument("--under-race", action="store_true",
                    help="with --curve: put BOTH triggers on the Racing effect "
                         "first, so the window is tested under an effect that "
                         "keeps the trigger analogue. Lock cannot answer this -- "
                         "it turns the axis into two states, leaving nothing for "
                         "a window to rescale")
    ap.add_argument("--lock", action="store_true",
                    help="put BOTH triggers on the Lock effect at --lock-at, and "
                         "give only the probed one a travel window ending there. "
                         "Answers two things at once: whether the window still "
                         "applies under an effect rather than only under "
                         "General, and whether a locked trigger can still report "
                         "full scale at the stop")
    ap.add_argument("--lock-at", type=int, default=128,
                    help="where the Lock effect stops the trigger, 20..200 "
                         "(default 128, mid-travel)")
    ap.add_argument("--baseline", action="store_true",
                    help="change nothing; just measure both triggers as they are")
    ap.add_argument("--side", choices=("left", "right"), default="left",
                    help="which trigger gets the window. The other is the control")
    ap.add_argument("--start", type=int, default=DEFAULT_START,
                    help=f"window start, 0..255 (default {DEFAULT_START})")
    ap.add_argument("--end", type=int, default=DEFAULT_END,
                    help=f"window end, 0..255 (default {DEFAULT_END}). The "
                         "default is deliberately degenerate -- see the module "
                         "docstring")
    ap.add_argument("--seconds", type=float, default=15.0)
    args = ap.parse_args()

    arms = [args.curve, args.param, args.lock, args.baseline]
    if sum(1 for a in arms if a) != 1:
        ap.error("pass exactly one of --curve, --param, --lock or --baseline")

    if args.baseline:
        seen, presses, ranges = sweep(args.seconds)
        left = describe("left", seen["left"], ranges["left"], presses)
        right = describe("right", seen["right"], ranges["right"], presses)
        print(f"\n{presses} button events during the window")
        print("\nBaseline only -- no window was written. These two counts are "
              "what a live block has to break.")
        return 0

    ctrl = device.Controller()
    # This probe writes a profile blob, and a bare Controller() is whichever
    # Flydigi pad answered first.
    try:
        identity.require(ctrl)
    except identity.WrongDevice as exc:
        sys.exit(f"error: {exc}")
    status = mapping.read_status(ctrl)
    if status is None:
        sys.exit("the pad did not answer -- press a button to wake it")
    cfg_id = status["active"]

    original = mapping.read_config(ctrl, cfg_id)
    edited = original.copy()

    was = {side: edited.trigger_effect(side)[0] for side in mapping.SIDES}
    other_side = "right" if args.side == "left" else "left"

    if args.lock:
        stop = max(20, min(200, args.lock_at))
        params, _bind = effects.stored(effects.MODE_LOCK, {"start": stop})
        for side in mapping.SIDES:
            edited.set_trigger_effect(side, effects.MODE_LOCK, params)
        # The control gets the factory window explicitly rather than whatever
        # was there, so the only difference between the two triggers is the one
        # being tested.
        edited.set_trigger_curve(other_side, zero=0, end=255)
        edited.set_trigger_curve(args.side, zero=0, end=stop)
        print(f"profile {cfg_id + 1}: both triggers -> Lock at {stop}; "
              f"{args.side} travel window 0..{stop}, {other_side} left at 0..255")
        print("pull BOTH triggers right up to the stop and hold there for part "
              "of the run -- the number that matters is how far each reports")
        print("do NOT force past the stop: the reading is what the pad reports "
              "at the lock, and overpowering it measures your grip instead")
        print("(applied only, never saved)")
        try:
            mapping.write_config(ctrl, cfg_id, edited, old=original)
            mapping.apply_config(ctrl, cfg_id)
            time.sleep(0.5)
            # The blob write alone engages nothing: Space Station follows every
            # applied-config read with a live command 81 per side, built from
            # the stored block (ControllerBusinessService.cs:1595 ->
            # UpdateAdapterTriggerConfig). One command per trigger -- a command
            # addressed to side 3 does nothing at all.
            for side_id in (device.SIDE_LEFT, device.SIDE_RIGHT):
                if not effects.lock(ctrl, side_id, stop):
                    sys.exit(f"the pad refused the lock on side {side_id}")
            print(f"both triggers locked at {stop} by a live command 81")
            seen, presses, ranges = sweep(args.seconds)
            probed = describe(args.side, seen[args.side], ranges[args.side],
                              presses)
            control = describe(other_side, seen[other_side], ranges[other_side],
                               presses)
            print(f"\n{presses} button events during the window")
            lock_verdict(probed, control, args.side, stop)
        finally:
            restore(ctrl, cfg_id, original, edited)
            ctrl.close()
        return 0

    if args.under_race and not args.curve:
        ap.error("--under-race goes with --curve")

    under = effects.MODE_RACE if args.under_race else effects.MODE_NORMAL
    race_params, _ = effects.stored(effects.MODE_RACE, {})
    for side in mapping.SIDES:
        # Both sides to the same effect, so a stored Lock or Race left over from
        # an earlier session cannot masquerade as a travel window. Under
        # General, params=None leaves the parameter slots alone -- which is what
        # the --param arm then writes into.
        edited.set_trigger_effect(side, under,
                                  race_params if args.under_race else None)

    start, end = sorted((max(0, min(255, args.start)),
                         max(0, min(255, args.end))))
    if args.curve:
        before = edited.trigger_curve(args.side)
        edited.set_trigger_curve(args.side, zero=start, end=end)
        where = (f"curve block at {mapping.OFF_TRIGGER_CURVE + mapping.SIDES.index(args.side) * mapping.CURVE_ENTRY}: "
                 f"zero {before['zero']} -> {start}, end {before['end']} -> {end} "
                 "(points mirrored onto the window)")
    else:
        base = mapping.OFF_FORCE_TRIGGER + mapping.SIDES.index(args.side) * 20
        _mode, params = edited.trigger_effect(args.side)
        before = list(params[:2])
        params = list(params)
        params[0], params[1] = start, end
        edited.set_trigger_effect(args.side, effects.MODE_NORMAL, params)
        where = (f"force-trigger Param[0..1] at {base + 10}/{base + 11}: "
                 f"{before} -> [{start}, {end}]")

    print(f"profile {cfg_id + 1}: effect {was['left']}/{was['right']} -> "
          f"General on both; {args.side} {where}")
    print(f"the {'right' if args.side == 'left' else 'left'} trigger is left "
          "alone and is the control -- squeeze both the same way")
    print("(applied only, never saved)")

    try:
        mapping.write_config(ctrl, cfg_id, edited, old=original)
        # The key table lands as the packet arrives, but nothing here is a key.
        # Apply the profile so the firmware re-parses the blocks that are, which
        # is what Space Station's own write does a moment later.
        mapping.apply_config(ctrl, cfg_id)
        time.sleep(0.5)
        # Live effect state survives a config apply, so an effect left running
        # by an earlier session would still be on the triggers. Clearing both is
        # also the faithful replay of General -- ForceTriggerConfigNormal is
        # exactly `[side, 0]`, one command per side.
        effects.clear_all(ctrl)
        if args.under_race:
            knobs = effects.defaults(effects.MODE_RACE)
            for side_id in (device.SIDE_LEFT, device.SIDE_RIGHT):
                if not effects.race(ctrl, side_id, knobs["start"],
                                    knobs["resistance"]):
                    sys.exit(f"the pad refused Racing on side {side_id}")
            print(f"both triggers on Racing (start {knobs['start']}, "
                  f"resistance {knobs['resistance']}) by a live command 81")
        seen, presses, ranges = sweep(args.seconds)
        probed = describe(args.side, seen[args.side], ranges[args.side], presses)
        control = describe(other_side, seen[other_side], ranges[other_side],
                           presses)
        print(f"\n{presses} button events during the window")
        verdict(probed, control, args.side)
    finally:
        restore(ctrl, cfg_id, original, edited)
        ctrl.close()
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
